speechtext:
Scientific officers may be all right in theory, but they do not know the history of the lobster along the Atlantic coast. The minister has not answered the difficulty I have raised in regard to the matter, and that is that the mother lobster is being destroyed. Through the hatchery method he says we preserved thirty per cent of the fry. Well, we are losing that thirty per cent now and the lobster industry is suffering to that extent. It is very easy for a professor to go into his office and make up his mind that the natural method is the best, and I agree with him, but he must have some regard for the fact that these female lobsters are being caught and destroyed. The whole purpose of building these hatcheries was to save those lobsters. My hon. friend should have consulted the
(Mr. Chisholm.}
House of Commons. There are a large number of men in this House representing Maritime constituencies who have had more experience in this matter than this professor at Kingston. But my hon. friend ignored the members of the House; he did not give them an opportunity of expressing an opinion in the matter before he came to a decision. If he had had the usual respect for the members of the House he would have come to them and told them that he was going to reverse a policy that had been followed for twenty-five years- that he was going to dispose of these hatcheries; then we would have been able to explain matters to him and perhaps cause him to change his mind. But he listened altogether to these scientific men who live a thousand miles from the coast and who do not know anything about the history of the lobster industry or of lobster breeding in that country. He did not even tell the members from the Maritime Provinces what he was going to do; he simply put the lobster hatcheries up to auction. Surely that course cannot be justified. The minister has to do more now; he has to show that we are saving that 30 per cent, and he has not been able to show it. My information is that we are losing and that the lobster industry is being depleted for want of the hatcheries.
